WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, several speakers

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.120
arrived at the same idea from completely different directions.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.360
Let's first hear from Don Syme of GitHub Next, who makes the case

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.840
that we've been missing a pillar all along.

5
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.160
It's not CI/CD, it's CI, CD and continuous AI.

6
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.040
We went back a year ago and we kind of did a conceptual project.

7
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.560
It's almost like a linguistic project in a way to say,

8
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.080
let's take the ethos

9
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.800
of continuous integration and continuous

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.280
deployment, and let's say, actually we were missing something all along.

11
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.120
There's a third pillar to that.

12
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.360
There's actually three.

13
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.520
It's not CI/CD, it's CI, CD and continuous AI.

14
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:55.520
And in a way, what do we mean by that?

15
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.960
Well, let's start to give that some, some, some.

16
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.080
If we're going to introduce a term like that, we have to give it some meaning.

17
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.560
Give it some body.

18
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.960
And when you start unpicking it,

19
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.800
The examples are absolutely everywhere.

20
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.400
And there are lots of people in the industry doing continuous AI,

21
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.320
making offerings about automated uses

22
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.760
of AI in our development processes.

23
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.040
And you see people coming up with flows.

24
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.120
Some of them are automated,

25
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.280
some of them are semi-automated about continuous documentation.

26
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.480
And then when you start to think about it, it begins to have

27
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.680
a different feel to individual productivity, a different emphasis.

28
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.000
The problem with piling everything into individual productivity is that all these

29
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.120
kind of conflicting kind of goals that the individual is under,

30
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.320
they have to be productive, but they also have to be quality.

31
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.320
They have to, they have to.

32
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.320
They've got to flow.

33
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.000
They have to serve the work queue

34
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.880
coming in, but they also have to work on one particular item.

35
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.720
And so the individual becomes a bit of a kind of crunch point

36
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.040
for all the information flows going through a software project. And

37
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.080
when you start to

38
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.240
think of things in continuous terms, you can start to unpick that a bit

39
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.720
and you can talk about things like, actually,

40
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.720
I would like to have continuous code improvement in my repository.

41
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.800
And, and, and it's actually real.

42
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.800
And if you want to get a feeling for what my kind of day is like

43
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.680
it's on my blog, you know, here's a typical morning.

44
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.360
I wake up to code improvements, start my day with code

45
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.560
that's better provided by my continuous improvement processes

46
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.600
so. We also heard from Paul Stack, who has taken this about as far as anyone.

47
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.160
Agents write every line. Five gates in CI.

48
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.400
The pull request is auto-merged. 217

49
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.600
issues shipped in 30 days by five people for about $3,000 a month.

50
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.120
When everything passes, it merges itself.

51
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.200
I'm not kidding. Okay.

52
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.200
The reason I'm not kidding is

53
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.080
if I show you

54
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.920
this pull request merged just before

55
00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.920
I got on the stage.

56
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.240
And you can see the pull request was opened by my agent.

57
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.240
It had the first review,

58
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.880
then this one passed.

59
00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.160
Then it got a big old block on review.

60
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.600
I can't do that.

61
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.600
The agent then figured out that it had to push some changes.

62
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.320
Right here.

63
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.120
Keeps going.

64
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.120
This is all context. It's incredibly important.

65
00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.240
There's the new change, and the agent was actually able to go

66
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.560
and reproduce itself, figure out that everything was fine.

67
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:43.960
These suggestions are all okay.

68
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.800
And then it auto-merged itself and kicked in. Okay.

69
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.640
We're not doing anything like out of the ordinary here.

70
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.840
We're just trusting a process that we continually hone in that process.

71
00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.120
So for us, the gate is our UAT.

72
00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.840
Just because something has merged doesn't mean it

73
00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.560
automatically gets to an end user.

74
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.880
We have this whole level of test that that goes through,

75
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:08.160
and we catch regressions every single day before it goes to end users.

76
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.040
Absolutely every single day. And it blocks our pipeline.

77
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.640
And nobody else can, like, publish a release out there until that's fixed.

78
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.280
Now the most important thing is here is in our UAT repo,

79
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.480
we have a line that says the tests are the source of truth.

80
00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.520
Don't change the tests.

81
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.680
Always figure out if it's a regression in the binary.

82
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.040
Okay?

83
00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.520
And what we've ended up with in the last 30 days is 295 issues have been opened.

84
00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:35.320
And that's issues.

85
00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.720
Not just bugs, but feature requests, etc.

86
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.800
I shipped 217 of them.

87
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.000
I closed 81 as either duplicates or just not things that we're going to do.

88
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.400
The median time to triage — and this is not work hours,

89
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.400
This is like elapsed hours in a day is 4.6 hours.

90
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.520
And from triage

91
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.800
the whole way through to ship, including all of those gates is 1.6 hours.

92
00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.440
Okay. There's five people.

93
00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.760
The company I work at, each of us has a Claude Code Max Pro,

94
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.760
which is $200, and then we spend about 1500 to $2000 per month.

95
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.880
On the whole CI review process.

96
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.920
We're able to ship software many times faster now

97
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.120
for $3,000 per month than we were ever able to do before.

98
00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.560
Patrick Debois is an AI product engineer at Tessl,

99
00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.800
and he's also the person who gave us the word DevOps.

100
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.760
He names the mistake almost everyone makes

101
00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.800
when the agent gets it wrong, we jump in and fix the code.

102
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.240
What we should be fixing is the system that produced it.

103
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.440
And I mentioned like build the thing that builds the thing.

104
00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.680
One of the

105
00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.960
mindset challenges that people have is even if the agent gets things wrong,

106
00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.320
they jump in and they correct a mistake.

107
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.920
What they really should be doing is improving the system

108
00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.440
that builds the code and improves that, so they don't have to do it again.

109
00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.880
So it's not about fix the code, it's fix the system.

110
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.360
And a lot of systems engineers and platform people, they think in system mentality.

111
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.960
And that's why it's also quite natural to kind of think in those terms.

112
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
It's for me right now in the industry still the like

113
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.640
one of the predominant mistakes

114
00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.800
is that you still go in and jump in and correct and take over.

115
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.360
The mentality should change to fix the system and have the agent flow to that.

116
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.680
So I mentioned it like what good looks like.

117
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.960
Claude learned that we should have

118
00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.960
planning before doing something.

119
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.360
We should have testing to see whether the agent

120
00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.560
did something well or not, or what do we pull the trick

121
00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.600
and change functionality that we didn't want?

122
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.560
I always say, like, I never knew

123
00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.080
I would see the day that developers would start writing documentation

124
00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.920
on their own, but they learned that actually by writing

125
00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.200
specs or whatever you call it, context, all of a sudden things become better.

126
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.240
And then if you add observability, the agents can actually

127
00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.360
kind of observe themselves and improve, right?

128
00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.040
Nothing new, it's just rehashed with the new mindset.

129
00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.080
It's not all the agents doing that stuff,

130
00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:18.480
okay.

131
00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.640
So that reusability comes into

132
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.760
not changing the context and the problems you think about.

133
00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.720
Reusable specs kind of make that and that goes to both harness components.

134
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.120
Tools make them reusable.

135
00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.920
Now, it's very tempting to not listen to whatever happens in the production.

136
00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.840
And yes, as a dev, you've never been used to like being told, like, you know,

137
00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.160
the only thing that matters is how your end user is having issues.

138
00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:49.240
And those have been signals that we've been trying to educate that

139
00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.240
like whenever you see a signal

140
00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.240
that is not performing well, change the system and improve that.

141
00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.240
So. And to close, Robert Overweg,

142
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.640
who built what he calls one brain for his whole company.

143
00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.040
Company knowledge.

144
00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.280
He can question from his phone in plain language.

145
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.080
And an agent that reads the internet overnight.

146
00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.760
So he wakes up to a briefing rather than a backlog.

147
00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:13.440
Right.

148
00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.760
I have sort of all, not all most of our company knowledge on my phone.

149
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.600
And I can just send voice messages, voice messages, or type myself

150
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.920
to surface the information, which is really, really valuable.

151
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.320
So one example here I can talk through natural language.

152
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.240
So I again forgot what we wanted to implement in our CI/CD pipeline.

153
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.040
And but I did

154
00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.320
remember that it was from Microsoft and they had like these four steps

155
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.160
or something.

156
00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.720
So I just asked my, my agent, what was that thing again

157
00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.680
for the, for CI/CD things.

158
00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.800
And then it says, oh, you probably mean

159
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.640
this and this and this and this, which was actually accurate.

160
00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:57.600
And then it also because it understands my context.

161
00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.560
It also asked, is this for the talk? Which is also really useful.

162
00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.600
So I just continuously have this sparring buddy.

163
00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.320
So what we're doing

164
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.400
is we no longer search for files, but more like for ideas and context

165
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.760
and those kind of things, which is a massive game changer.

166
00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.520
Then we have agents as well.

167
00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.440
So I have an agent that proactively does search for me,

168
00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.920
and I have an agent that is able to interpret search that I find myself.

169
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.040
I want to show some examples here as well.

170
00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.560
Maybe you guys have this already, right?

171
00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.840
And this is really also quite basic, but we have a cron job

172
00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.160
or I have a cron job that runs on my OpenClaw that tracks

173
00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.320
certain accounts on X, those accounts,

174
00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:43.040
just, on just some specific words so

175
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.400
you can think of agentic engineering or, or any words

176
00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.400
that relate to such pipelines that I'm interested in.

177
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.000
I follow some niche accounts as well. Or niche.

178
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.720
I think a guy has maybe like 120K followers, maybe not that niche,

179
00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:02.520
but I do stay in in touch with everything that is happening.

180
00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.360
And it's almost like my newspaper because I don't read the newspaper,

181
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.760
but I read everything.

182
00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:12.240
evolving around agents and AI and those kind of things.

183
00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.920
And that's being surfaced to me, daily in the morning.

184
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.080
Thanks for watching.

185
00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:23.720
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

186
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:27.680
